<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nagas of Padmavati</h1><p class="st-meta">150 CE – 350 CE (approx.) · capital Padmavati</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Naga dynasty of central India, ruling from Padmavati near Gwalior between the Kushans and the Guptas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, several Naga ruling families emerged in central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 2nd and 3rd centuries. The best known ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawaya">Padmavati</a>, modern Pawaya near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a>, with other branches at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>. Many scholars identify them with the Bharashivas of Vakataka records.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Nagas are known from many small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins and a few inscriptions. Vakataka inscriptions say the Bharashiva kings carried a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> linga on their shoulders and performed ten horse sacrifices and were anointed with the waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, suggesting strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> devotion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhava-naga">Bhava Naga</a> married his daughter to the Vakataka house, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudrasena-i">Rudrasena I</a> became a Vakataka king.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 4th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> defeated Naga rulers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganapati-naga">Ganapati Naga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a>, and absorbed their lands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> then married into the Naga family through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II's</a> queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuberanaga">Kuberanaga</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nagas-of-padmavati.jpg" alt="Nagas of Padmavati" width="320" height="188"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lessman (Contact!) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East-Hem_300ad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>150 CE – 350 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Padmavati</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhava Naga, Ganapati Naga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Naga dynasty, Bharashivas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=250&amp;focus=nagas-of-padmavati" aria-label="Nagas of Padmavati on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6763 1345 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nagas of Padmavati lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1168h0M7362 1550h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7083 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7083 1601h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=250&amp;focus=nagas-of-padmavati">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
